What is a Dummy Node?

A dummy node is an extra node created before the actual head of a linked list.

It does not store real data
It helps simplify edge cases
Especially useful when head might change
ListNode dummy = new ListNode(0);
dummy.next = head;

🔸 Why Use Dummy Node?

Without dummy node, you must handle:
Removing the head
Inserting at the start
Empty list checks

With dummy node:
✅ Same logic for all nodes
✅ Cleaner code
✅ Fewer bugs

🔸 When Should You Use It?

Use Dummy Node Technique when:

Head may be deleted
Head may be inserted before
Multiple pointer updates are needed
You want to avoid special if (head == ...) cases

🔸 Core Idea
dummy → head → node1 → node2 → ...

You never lose the head, because:
Return dummy.next
Head can safely change

Dummy Node Technique Checklist
✔ Use Dummy Node When:

 Head may change
 Deletion is involved
 Insertion before head is possible
 You want uniform logic

✔ Steps to Follow

 Create dummy node
 Point dummy.next → head
 Use a pointer starting at dummy
 Perform operations normally
 Return dummy.next